Date: Thu, 11 Mar 93 13:08:07 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Extropianism as a Cargo Cult

Extropianism, like its cousin, Transhumanism, is a powerful attractor of
those seeking something beyond themselves. 

>From my Random House Unabridged, 2nd ed.:

cargo cult, (sometimes caps.) any of various native religious cults of a
millenarian and messianic character located in the southwestern Pacific
islands, holding that spirits will bring large cargoes of modern goods for
distribution among its adherents. [1945-50]

Rand and others used to use this term a lot, back in the 1950s. There was
probably some big "Life" magazine article on cargo cults which led to the
wide use of the term--it doesn't matter.

What matters is that Extropianism and nanotechnology (the key enabling
technology for nearly all core Extropian goals) have become cargo cults for
various fringe believers:

-believe in nanotech and you will get unlimited abundance.

-believe in Extropianism and reincarnation will be yours.

-become a cryonicist and you will live forever.

-subscribe to the list and flying yogis will finally get their recognition.

-send messages to the list about your psychic disruption systems and CBS
will finally take you seriously.

-explain to the list how chanting in the forest and dancing around a fire
with other Seekers is actually a memetic link to your Aryan ancestor
spirits.

-tell the list how Christians and other "Yahweh" worshippers are evil and
irrational, but Goddess worshippers and tree-worshipping Pagans are good
and level-headed ("Many of us are programmers").

...And so on, ad nauseum.

Now some of these items are possible, even perhaps likely (someday). Some
of the ideas, like nanotech, AI, cryonics, etc., have plausible scientific
bases and possible economic/technological development paths. Others are
patently false and can be demonstrated to be so. And yet the noise
continues.

The wishful thinking and willingness to support bizarre, pseudoscientific
ideas is truly astounding to me.

There are far more nutcases on this list than I've seen anywhere else,
except perhaps Mensa and the Society for Creative Anachronism.

Cheers.

-Tim May
