Subject: PHIL: Reincarnation vs. Migration to Silicon

The recent reply of Perry Metzger to Carol Moore on this thread:

[Carol]

>> 
>> It suddenly occured to me -- why is the idea of
>> humans storing or transfering their consciousness to
>> silicon chips taken seriously in some circles,
>> while the idea of transfering consciousness to another
>> physical being is not?

[Perry]

>Because one of them has a serious proposed engineering solution, and
>the other is, in my personal opinion, a notion without any relationship
>to reality. As an example, you might ask yourself why it is that people
>believe it is possible to fly with the aid of an airplane, but consider
>the notion of flying by jumping off a cliff and waving one's arms vigorously
>to be somewhat on the specious side.

But if you think about it, human motivations and memes alike come 
about because people *want* something--like flight or some comfort of 
not dying, even when their body is gone.  Like Perry, I am more 
inclined to engineering as a way to satisfy wants rather than the 
mystical.  (This only makes sense when science and engineering are
up to the task, or approaching it.)

By the way, Carol, if we can transfer consciousness to silicon, we 
almost certainly can transfer it to another body.  I certainly don't 
want to restart the massive threads which ran last year on 
duplication, but eventually reincarnation (reanimation from a backup) 
will be a service you can get on contract.   

Keith

