Date: Thu, 11 Mar 93 16:01:53 EST
From: Brian.Hawthorne@East.Sun.COM (Brian Holt Hawthorne - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: PHIL: Reincarnation vs. Migration to Silicon

 
Carol Moore:
> > It suddenly occured to me -- why is the idea of
> > humans storing or transfering their consciousness to
> > silicon chips taken seriously in some circles,
> > while the idea of transfering consciousness to another
> > physical being is not?

Perry:
> Because one of them has a serious proposed engineering solution

Although I at first found myself agreeing with Perry, I don't think
it is this clear-cut. Since we do not currently have any serious
engineering description of just what it is that makes human consciousness
(unless someone solved that problem while my back was turned), I
would have to be quite liberal in my interpretation of 'engineering solution'
to accept Perry's thesis.

Now, I have my pet theories and models of how human consciousness works, but
they are only slightly based on objective empirical evidence derived using
the scientific method.

I guess my answer to Carol is:

Even the small amount of evidence we have suggests that human 
consciousness is an emergent property of a particular physical
system (the human nervous system with its peripheral devices).
While we don't have a complete model, we have not yet encountered
anything that leads us to believe that this approach will be
unsuccessful. If we succeed in determining how this property
emerges from the underlying system, it will be conceptually possible
to duplicate that emergence on a different system.

So, why does this make uploading likely and reincarnation unlikely?
The emergent property of consciousness represents
an enormous amount of information about the underlying system.
In order to transfer this information to another system, you 
can either create a system from scratch, or rearrange an existing
system. Either could work for uploading, reincarnation assumes the
former (unless you allow overlap in incarnations). So, where do
you store the information between incarnations? What mechanism
do you use to ensure that the new system gets organized according
to the information you have stored?

In other words, I suspect you could get reincarnation to work, just
as you could get uploading to work, but it requires someone to actively
do it. We (pardon me while I speak for others) do find the likelihood
of migration to other HNS unlikely, merely that it is going on right
now, all by itself, completely unnoticed by any scientific means.

	Rowan

