Date: Thu, 11 Mar 93 15:15:37 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Extropianism as a Cargo Cult

Let me clarify that I was emphatically *not* saying that either nanotech or
cryonics is bogus. Rather, I was pointing out that many "true believers" in
these and related areas have agendas more closely related to "yoga science"
and reincarnationism than to the scientific or engineering side.

Derek Zahn writes (quoting me):

>> -believe in Extropianism and reincarnation will be yours.
>
>But nobody said that (except for a largely non-serious body replacement
>comparison with religious ideas about reincarnation).  The reincarnationists'
>beliefs appear to be orthogonal to any extropian concerns...

I agree, but some are taking from Extropianism what they wish--hence the
cargo cult aspect--without regard for plausibility.

>> -become a cryonicist and you will live forever.
>
>Who was saying that?  As I recall, the point of the cryonicists
>is "some chance of surviving deanimation is better than none".

Again, I'm not faulting the science and social aspects of cryonics (some of
my best friends are signed up with Alcor, and I may sign up too). Rather,
the gee-whiz pseudoscience. The confusion some have between
reincarnation--past lives and all that mystical crap--and reanimation is
telling.

>I fear that any list like this, with a rather broad focus, will
>produce noise of the type you mention.  What astounds me is the
>patience taken by many to reply to the noise in a thoughtful way.

In my less than humble opinion, the tolerance we have generally shown to
fringe believers, tree worshipping Pagans, yogic pilots, psychics, and
other cargo cultists has caused them to *exploit* the list. By having our
"no flaming" rule, admirable as it is, we have no effective means of
driving out the "rationally-challenged."

(Anybody tuning in this list, trying it out, would have to conclude that
Extropianism consists of basically two categories: extreme
anarchocapitalist libertarians and neo-Pagan Wiccan mystics. If I weren't
already an extreme anarchocapitalist libertarian, I doubt I'd stay for
long. Food for thought.)

Some of the more ludicrous--but obviously not satirical--postings from Jeff
Harrington and Pandit Singh are, in my opinion, taunts. Like a child
taunting caged animals, they know we are powerless to flame them. And so
they persist.

Frankly, I'm surprised at how rude some folks are to the "socialists" who
appear on this list, while being so polite to the "mystics." At least
socialism is "arguable," i.e., I think the Marxist or socialist view of
human nature and economics is flawed, but I don't automatically assume
their points are automatically ignorable. (It is true that a couple of
times the mystics have gotten flamed by long term list members. One of the
mystics filed a complaint with the list management and various punishments
were meted out--I forget who got the heavier punishment, though.)

I say it's time we stand up for rational principles and some trust (I dare
not say "faith") in the scientific method.

-Tim
